  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Severe and flash flooding threat has largely \ndiminished this evening with no widespread hazardous weather \nexpected tonight.\n\nCurrent radar outlines a cluster of showers with some isolated\nlightning strikes across northern Indiana County with scattered\nshowers behind this cluster that will continue to move into\nClearfield/Cambria counties over the next hour. Severe threat\nhas largely waned given stability across the region despite the\nfavorable shear parameters based on the most recent SPC \nMesoanalysis parameters. Flooding threat also remains fairly \nlow across the region with the only area of real concern given\nthe overall lack of widespread heavy rainfall this afternoon and\nevening and a fall off of rainfall rates overall. Some potential\nfor fog formation across the higher elevations of western\nPennsylvania where heavy rainfall was observed today; however,\nthe signal is not super strong at this time with overall dew\npoint depressions still having a bit of ways to fall before fog\nformation occurs tonight. If fog forms across higher elevations,\nrapidly changing driving conditions could be present across\nportions of the Allegheny Front into Wednesday morning.\n\n     --------------------------------------------------\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Outside of some strong-to-severe storms across \nLancaster County on Wednesday, generally tranquil weather \nexpected through the end of the work week.\n\nSurface cold front will continue to push eastward across central\nPennsylvania tomorrow morning and throughout the afternoon \nhours, which will bring some potential for showers and storms to\nLancaster County during the late afternoon hours depending on\nthe speed of the cold frontal passage. More recent NBM guidance\nhas trended back on precipitation mentions across the region and\nthis is shown in the most recent forecast. If the cold front\ntrends slower, it appears there would be enough moisture and\npotentially shear in place to bring a threat for marginally\nstrong-to-severe thunderstorms. It is of note that instability \nwill likely not be super sufficient for severe thunderstorms \ngiven morning cloud cover in place across the region, which\ncould tamper the severe threat should the slower cold frontal\npassage solution play out in real time.\n\nDry conditions prevail Wednesday evening and are generally\nexpected to continue through the Saturday evening based on most\nrecent NBM model guidance. Towards the latter half of the\nweekend and into the beginning of next week, overall an\nunsettled pattern appears to take hold of the region with\nshowers and chances of afternoon/evening thunderstorms progged\nby NBM model guidance. Despite this, no large-scale signals for\nsevere weather at this time.",
